 ## Changelog
+- 2026-10-23: ScalableLux port (ScalableLux 26.2.0 = Starlight + parallel light updates, LGPL-3.0; read `README.md`, `TECHNICAL_DETAILS.md`, `StarLightEngine`, `BlockStarLightEngine`, `SkyStarLightEngine`, `StarLightInterface`, `Config`, `GlobalExecutors`). New std-only workspace crate `starlight/` (`COPYING*` from ScalableLux, same text as `embeddium/`); `world/chunks/light.rs` keeps the light thread and its message protocol and hands the work to it. Verified: `cargo test -p starlight` (2 tests) passes on a Linux host with rustc 1.99; `cargo check --lib --tests` for aarch64-linux-android passes; the `LightWorld`-level mc-rs tests (`settled_light_is_the_plain_formula`, `chunk_layout_matches_the_engine`) and the thread protocol were run in a scratch crate on the same sources. NOT run: the `ChunkManager`-level tests in `light.rs` (`light_thread_matches_the_engine`, `light_settles_after_edits`: the cdylib cannot be built on a host), anything on a device.
+    - What is ported: the propagator (`performLightIncrease` / `performLightDecrease`: one FIFO each per kind, entries with a direction bitset so the cell a level came from is not read again, nothing below level 2 queued, `WRITE` / `RECHECK` flags), `checkBlock` and `calculateLightValue`, the stateless sky sources (the column's source run is brought in line with its height by scanning the light, like `tryPropagateSkylight`; the first light of a chunk queues only the source cells beside a darker column), `checkChunkEdges` (both sides of a seam with a lit neighbour, wrong cells collected first and then checked) and ScalableLux's parallel updates (`run`: waves of up to `default_workers()` tasks whose 3x3 chunk windows are disjoint, a window is moved out of the map for the task so there is no lock, scoped threads, one task runs inline). `default_workers()` is ScalableLux's `Config.PARALLELISM` default, cores / 3 at least 1.
+    - What changed around it: `Msg::Edit` writes the block and the height at once and only queues the cell in the chunk's `Task` (an edit of an unlit chunk is dropped: its first light reads the blocks as they are), so all edits of a chunk that piled up are one pass; a chunk that is not lit is absent for the engine (nothing is written into it); `Region`, `update_region`, `relit`, `schedule_light`, `schedule_lateral`, `relight_column`, `init_chunk` and `mark_dirty` are gone (the stale / touched flags are set where the light is written, the neighbour across a border included).
+    - Why it is the same light: both compute `L = max(source, max(neighbours) - max(opacity, 1))` (sky source `y >= height`, block source the emission). `starlight::tests::settled_light_is_the_fixpoint` compares the settled light with a flood-fill brute force over random terrain (stone, caves, leaves, water, glowstone, torches) after batches of first lights in random order, random edits and a re-light of an already lit chunk, with 1 and 3 workers; three bugs injected on purpose (no scan for a covered column, no edge checks, no re-propagation of brighter neighbours in a decrease) each make it fail.
+    - Measured on the Linux host (x86_64, release, not a phone; 9 x 9 chunks of hilly terrain with trees, water, torches and caves; old engine = the repo as uploaded, both on the same light-thread-less `LightWorld`): first light of 81 chunks 52 ms -> 35 ms (1 worker; 3 workers 39 ms, no gain: waves of 3 are rare while every task is a first light), 300 single edits one run each 84 ms -> 9 ms, 300 edits queued and run once 9 ms. The old engine left 3 stale water cells in the interior (`want 9, got 12` under water), the new one none: every interior cell obeys the formula.
+    - ponytail: a wave waits for its slowest task (ScalableLux's per-chunk lock tokens on a persistent pool have no barrier; upgrade path: a pool plus per-chunk busy flags); `Arc::make_mut` runs per light write, an atomic check each (unshare once per touched chunk); `light_chunk` reads all 32768 blocks for emitters (Starlight skips sections whose palette has none; a per-chunk emitter count would do); the 3x3 window is enough because light never goes further than 15 cells and a chunk is 16 wide; unloading a chunk leaves the light it gave its neighbours until the next edit near it (same as vanilla). Not ported: `SWMRNibbleArray` (null / zero / full nibble states: a flat array per chunk here), the vanilla light interface, the save format (the game does not save light), conditionally transparent blocks (none in b1.7.3), section emptiness maps. No option for the thread count (the const lives in `default_workers()`).
+    - UNVERIFIED on a device: frame time and the first-light time on a phone, that the parallel waves help there (cores / 3 is 1 on a 4-core phone, so no thread is spawned), and the look of the meshes after an edit (stale flags are set where the old `mark_dirty` set them).
 
 - 2026-10-23: C2ME's density function compiler ideas in the `tectonic` crate (`c2me-opts-dfc`, MIT; ideas only, no code copied) (written WITHOUT a Rust toolchain: not compiled, not run; +3 tests `tectonic::tests::{simplified_graph_gives_the_plain_graphs_bits, simplify_drops_the_wrappers, memo_follows_the_column_and_the_point}`, not run; the existing `chunk_matches_the_python_reference` must still pass). `OpenCL` module of C2ME is All Rights Reserved and needs f64, which GLES lacks: not used.
     - Before: `Tectonic::ev` kept y-independent values in a `HashMap<(node, x, z), f64>` (SipHash) and recomputed every y-dependent node on every visit. Counted, not measured: pass 2 asks for the root on 32768 blocks, and the part of the root outside the one `interpolated` node is 8 nodes (3 y-independent, no noise), so the map was probed ~100k times a chunk; the noise work is in pass 1 (425 points of the `interpolated` argument, `base_3d_noise` is 40 octaves of each).
